WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:04.825
Here's a line from a build file.

2
00:00:04.825 --> 00:00:10.448
It uses a ready-made step that another project wrote, called an action, at version v4.

3
00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:11.755
v4 is v4, right?

4
00:00:11.755 --> 00:00:13.415
So here's the question.

5
00:00:13.415 --> 00:00:18.396
If nobody touches this file, can tomorrow's build run different code?

6
00:00:18.396 --> 00:00:20.056
Hold on to your answer.

7
00:00:21.133 --> 00:00:22.495
Back to our jam shop.

8
00:00:22.495 --> 00:00:28.592
In the pantry there are sacks of sugar, and your recipe card says: use the sack labelled good.

9
00:00:28.733 --> 00:00:31.888
A helper ties the label to the first sack.

10
00:00:31.888 --> 00:00:32.564
You cook.

11
00:00:32.564 --> 00:00:33.916
The jam is lovely.

12
00:00:34.067 --> 00:00:40.132
Overnight, someone with a key to the pantry swaps what's in the second sack, and moves the label onto it.

13
00:00:40.267 --> 00:00:43.738
In the morning you follow the recipe card, word for word.

14
00:00:43.738 --> 00:00:45.443
Which sack do you cook with?

15
00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:46.415
The bad one.

16
00:00:46.415 --> 00:00:48.522
Your card didn't change at all.

17
00:00:48.522 --> 00:00:52.124
It never named a sack, only a label, and labels move.

18
00:00:52.124 --> 00:00:53.687
The recipe was perfect.

19
00:00:53.687 --> 00:00:56.065
It just pointed at the wrong sugar.

20
00:00:57.067 --> 00:00:59.920
Now the real thing, with a tiny experiment.

21
00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:04.698
Git keeps a project's history as commits, saved snapshots of every file.

22
00:01:04.698 --> 00:01:07.220
Each commit is one sack in the pantry.

23
00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:13.094
The maintainer, the person who looks after the action, saves this commit and tags it v1.

24
00:01:13.094 --> 00:01:18.464
A tag is the label: a name that points at one commit, like a sticky note on a page.

25
00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:22.820
And your workflow, the file of build steps, is the recipe card.

26
00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:23.758
It says at v1.

27
00:01:23.758 --> 00:01:26.705
It follows the label, wherever the label is.

28
00:01:26.867 --> 00:01:30.584
Now play the attacker, holding the maintainer's stolen token.

29
00:01:30.584 --> 00:01:34.240
Change the code, save a new commit, with a friendly message.

30
00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.664
Then one command moves the tag.

31
00:01:36.664 --> 00:01:38.125
Minus f means force.

32
00:01:38.267 --> 00:01:40.194
Your workflow still says v1.

33
00:01:40.194 --> 00:01:42.604
Which code runs in your next build?

34
00:01:42.604 --> 00:01:43.705
Pause and guess.

35
00:01:45.867 --> 00:01:46.789
The attacker's.

36
00:01:46.789 --> 00:01:52.387
The tag moved, and your build went with it, though not one letter of your own file changed.

37
00:01:52.533 --> 00:01:54.837
So the answer to our question is yes.

38
00:01:54.837 --> 00:01:58.199
v4 reads like a fixed version, but it's a sticky note.

39
00:01:58.199 --> 00:02:02.495
Anyone who holds the key can peel it off and stick it somewhere else.

40
00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:05.525
This isn't a thought experiment.

41
00:02:05.525 --> 00:02:11.775
In March 2025, attackers moved the tags of a popular action, tj-actions changed-files.

42
00:02:11.775 --> 00:02:18.896
For about a day, every workflow using one of those tags printed its CI secrets into the build log.

43
00:02:18.896 --> 00:02:22.384
Over twenty-three thousand repositories used it.

44
00:02:22.533 --> 00:02:29.280
And on the nineteenth of March 2026, attackers moved 76 of the 77 tags of trivy-action.

45
00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:34.088
That's a security scanner, the very tool meant to protect you.

46
00:02:35.067 --> 00:02:38.640
Wouldn't it be nice to name the sack itself, not its label?

47
00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.608
Git gives every commit exactly that kind of name.

48
00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:50.356
A commit SHA is a hash, a fingerprint worked out from the commit's content: its files, its parent commit, its author and its message.

49
00:02:50.356 --> 00:02:52.672
That gives forty letters and digits.

50
00:02:52.867 --> 00:02:56.555
Change one byte, and you get a completely different fingerprint.

51
00:02:56.555 --> 00:03:00.475
The attacker's commit has its own, and can never borrow the old one.

52
00:03:00.667 --> 00:03:02.949
No real sack of sugar works like that.

53
00:03:02.949 --> 00:03:08.775
Imagine a label printed from the sugar itself, where swapping one grain prints a different label.

54
00:03:08.775 --> 00:03:13.039
That's where the pantry story stops, and git is better than the pantry.

55
00:03:13.733 --> 00:03:15.913
And the old commit is still there.

56
00:03:15.913 --> 00:03:22.067
Ask git for it by its fingerprint, and you get the old, harmless code, even after the tag moved.

57
00:03:23.067 --> 00:03:26.374
So pin the step to the fingerprint instead of the label.

58
00:03:26.374 --> 00:03:27.968
This is called SHA pinning.

59
00:03:27.968 --> 00:03:33.519
A workflow pinned like this keeps running exactly the code you reviewed, wherever the tags go.

60
00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:38.388
Here's the real scan step in the lab's release workflow.

61
00:03:38.388 --> 00:03:42.875
Notice: it's the same scanner whose tags were moved in 2026.

62
00:03:43.067 --> 00:03:44.359
GitHub runs the SHA.

63
00:03:44.359 --> 00:03:51.016
The part after the hash sign is a comment, a note for humans, so you can still see which release it is.

64
00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:52.369
Is there a catch?

65
00:03:52.369 --> 00:03:54.364
Yes, and it's fair to say it.

66
00:03:54.364 --> 00:03:58.836
Pinned code never changes by itself, not even to bring you a fix.

67
00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:00.973
So you need a bot and a person.

68
00:04:00.973 --> 00:04:07.273
Dependabot, GitHub's robot that watches for new releases, updates the SHA and the comment together.

69
00:04:07.273 --> 00:04:10.583
And someone reviews that change like any other code.

70
00:04:11.533 --> 00:04:14.591
Images have the same two kinds of name.

71
00:04:14.591 --> 00:04:20.549
A tag such as hello-devops colon 1.2.0 can be pushed again with new content.

72
00:04:20.733 --> 00:04:22.427
A digest can't change.

73
00:04:22.427 --> 00:04:29.045
It's at sha256, then sixty-four letters and digits, a fingerprint of the image itself.

74
00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:32.332
And that explains a detail from the Trivy attack.

75
00:04:32.332 --> 00:04:35.016
Images pulled by digest were not affected.

76
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:37.846
So the moral of the story.

77
00:04:37.846 --> 00:04:41.184
A tag can be moved by anyone who holds the key.

78
00:04:41.184 --> 00:04:42.036
Pin the SHA.

79
00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:44.165
A label only points at a sack.

80
00:04:44.165 --> 00:04:46.523
A fingerprint names the sack itself.

81
00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:48.529
A quick check.

82
00:04:48.529 --> 00:04:52.046
Your workflow pins docker/login-action to a full SHA.

83
00:04:52.046 --> 00:04:56.890
Tonight, someone takes over the maintainers' account and moves every tag.

84
00:04:56.890 --> 00:04:58.881
What runs in tomorrow's build?

85
00:04:58.881 --> 00:04:59.943
Pause and think.

86
00:05:02.133 --> 00:05:04.234
The same reviewed code as before.

87
00:05:04.234 --> 00:05:07.352
Moving tags can't change what a pinned step runs.
